New vocab for me:

- "the palsy" is an old term that refers to paralysis or loss of muscle control. Would have encompassed modern day ailments like stroke or muscular disorders which can result in paralysis 
- sat at "meat" is old language for having a meal. Meat referred to any type food or nourishment, not just animal flesh
- a "dumb man" means a person who is mute or unable to speak, rather than someone lacking intelligence



Reflections:

- Take aways for me from this chapter:

Matthew was a tax collector which would have made him super wealthy and comfortable in those times while also being hated by Jews for extorting them on behalf of the roman empire. Regadless, When Jesus walks by and says "follow me"
Matthew doesn't hesistate. The message there: radical change is possible through Jesus. Once he changes your heart, life can immediately and radically tranform as a result

Another thing I thought was interesting was that after Jesus cast out demons and healed the blind men, the Pharisees who were the religious leaders of the time, resorted to manipulating what had happened to slander Jesus saying that 
he casts out demons through the prince of demons. Couldn't attack the deeds so they twisted facts and attacked the man. Still happens a lot today. People cant attack ideas so they attack and slander people in a desperate attempt to
throw hate. Keep watch for that

Somethiung I learned recently is that The Greek word with the root meaning "slanderer" is diábolos (διάβολος), which is actually the root word for the Devil, 


